      "id": 22,
      "title": "How 3D is transforming pediatric orthopedic surgery",
      "url": "https://medicine.yale.edu/ortho/news-article/how-3d-is-transforming-pediatric-orthopedic-surgery/",
      "summary": "A Yale team has developed a way to create a precise virtual replica of a child’s bone, map out limb reconstruction surgery virtually, and use custom-printed tools to execute the plan with unprecedented precision.",
      "content": "How 3D is Transforming Pediatric Orthopedic Surgery | Orthopædics & Rehabilitation Skip to Main Content Orthopædics & Rehabilitation MENU Donate to Orthopaedic Research Yale School of Medicine MENU About Collaborative Excellence Staff Support Us Patient Care Research Publications Faculty Specialties Clinical Research Avascular Necrosis Clinical Trial Liu Lab for Translational Orthopaedic Research Selected Publications Our Team Biomechanics Laboratory 3D Collaborative for Medical Innovation (3DC) Histology and Histomorphometry Laboratory Our Services Methylmethacrylate Embedding (MMA) Glycolmethacrylate Embedding (GMA) Paraffin Embedding Frozen Sections (Cryotomy) Histomorphometry Special Stains Digital Photography Request Service Faculty & Staff References 3D Orthopaedics Lab Our Team Avascular Necrosis Hand & Upper Extremity Hip-Spine Biomechanics Medical Device Design Orthopaedic Trauma Patellofemoral Research Program Rehabilitation Engineering Faculty & APPs Education Residencies Orthopaedic Surgery Residency Program Structure Curriculum Training Sites Grand Rounds Residents Resident Life Fellowship Placements Physical Medicine & Rehabilitation Residency Residents Fellowships Arthroplasty Fellowship Fellowship Alumni Keggi International Scholars Program Orthopaedic Trauma Fellowship Meet the Fellowship Faculty Your Experience Why Choose Yale? Apply Spine Fellowship Sports Medicine Fellowship Meet the Clinical Experts Your Experience Why an Academic Medical Center? Why Choose Yale? Apply Medical Students and Sub-Interns Southwick Society MS in Personalized Medicine & Applied Engineering News & Events Events Calendar Divisions Foot & Ankle Patient Care Publications Hand & Upper Extremity Patient Care Publications Hip & Knee Joint Reconstruction Patient Care Kristaps Juris Keggi, M.D. In Memoriam Publications Musculoskeletal Oncology Patient Care Publications Pediatrics Patient Care Publications Physical Medicine & Rehabilitation Patient Care Publications Spine Patient Care Publications Sports Medicine Patient Care Publications Trauma Patient Care Publications YSM Home INFORMATION FOR About YSM Faculty Staff Students Residents & Fellows Patients Researchers Alumni 3 Minute Read Share article X Logo Twitter Streamline Icon: https://streamlinehq.com Social Media Facebook Streamline Icon: https://streamlinehq.com Professional Network Linkedin Streamline Icon: https://streamlinehq.com Email Action Unread Streamline Icon: https://streamlinehq.com Print Text Streamline Icon: https://streamlinehq.com For decades, orthopedic surgeons relied on two-dimensional X-rays to repair three-dimensional problems. While effective for simple broken bones, this traditional approach can be like trying to solve a Rubik’s Cube while only looking at one side. New technology is changing pediatric limb reconstruction. A January 2026 article published in Orthopedic Clinics showed how a Yale team can create a precise virtual replica of a child’s bone, map out the surgery virtually, and use custom-printed tools to execute the plan with unprecedented precision thanks to advances in 3D planning and patient-specific instrumentation. Precision medicine for growing bodies Children present a unique challenge in orthopaedics. Their bones are still growing, and their joints are governed by delicate growth plates. Damaging these areas during surgery can lead to lifelong complications. \"Digital modeling allows us to see the hidden complexities of a child's anatomy,\" says David Frumberg, MD , associate professor of orthopaedics and rehabilitation at Yale School of Medicine and the study's principal investigator. “The process involves taking high-resolution CT scans and converting them into digital 3D models. We can then determine the best way to perform surgery on a computer multiple times before the patient even enters the operating room.” This enables orthopedic surgeons to find the best angle in an osteotomy, for example, a procedure where a bone is cut and reshaped to correct a deformity. Once a virtual plan is perfected, engineers use medical-grade 3D printers to create patient-specific instrumentation. These are custom surgical guides designed to precisely fit onto a specific patient’s bone. These guides tell the surgeon exactly where to cut and where to place screws, removing guesswork and margins for error that can come with freehand techniques. According to Frumberg, research shows that this level of accuracy leads to: Shorter operative times: In some cases, surgery time was cut in half because the major decisions were made during the planning phase. Less radiation: Because the guides provide a roadmap, surgeons need fewer intra-operative X-rays to check their work. Better outcomes: Custom guides help ensure that the limb is aligned perfectly, which is critical for long-term joint health. Saving an ankle joint The power of this technology is best seen in complex cases where standard methods might not be enough. The case documented by Frumberg and his team in the paper highlights a 13-year-old boy with a severe deformity in his lower tibia caused by a rare medical condition. “Traditional options for surgical correction might have otherwise required fusing his ankle joint—a move that would stop the pain but severely limit his mobility,” Frumberg says. “Instead, we used 3D planning to create a joint-preserving strategy. By using 3D-printed guides, we were able to reshape the inside of the ankle joint and restore its natural alignment. As a result, the young patient regained mobility and returned to the activities he loved.” Having a pre-printed \"step-by-step\" visual guide helps the entire surgical team stays in sync, making the procedure safer and more predictable, says Frumberg. Beyond the clinical results, he adds, studies also suggest that reducing time in the operating room can save thousands of dollars per case. The future of orthopedic innovation While 3D printing in medicine began with simple plastic models, it has evolved into a sophisticated multidisciplinary field. At leading institutions like Yale, surgeons and engineers work side-by-side and in new ways to solve the toughest cases. “As the technology becomes more accessible, it is expected to move from a specialized tool for complex deformities to a standard of care for many orthopedic procedures,” Frumberg says. “The goal of using 3D surgical planning is simple: improving the lives of children through better technology. More broadly speaking, it means a surgery is less about ‘one size fits all’ and more about a custom-tailored path to an individual’s recovery.” Article outro Author John Ready, MS Communications Officer Media Contact For media inquiries, please contact us. ysmmedia@yale.edu Explore More Copy Link Featured in this article David Frumberg, MD, FAAOS Associate Professor of Orthopaedics and Rehabilitation Kinjal Vasavada, MD Hospital Resident Alyssa Glennon Program Director and Lead Engineer, 3D Collaborative for Medical Innovation Related Links Video: Why Virtual 3D Planning is Crucial to Orthopedic Surgery at Yale Video: 3D Modeling for Orthopedic Surgical Precision - Amelia's Story Yale Begins Fully in-House 3D Orthopedic Surgical Procedures Back to Top Your browser is antiquated and no longer supported on this website. Please update your browser or switch to Chrome, Firefox or Safari. You can update your IE here: https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/help/17621/internet-explorer-downloads",
